//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Politics_in_education","c":[]},{"n":"Process evaluation","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Educational_evaluation","c":[]},{"n":"Psychology","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Educational_psychology","c":[]},{"n":"Sociology","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sociology_of_education","c":[]}]},{"n":"Educational research","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Educational_research","c":[]},{"n":"List of education journals","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_education_journals","c":[]}]},{"n":"Educational scholars and researchers","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outline_of_education#Educational_scholars_and_researchers","c":[{"n":"Category:Education activists","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Education_activists","c":[]},{"n":"Category:Education writers","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Education_writers","c":[{"n":"Category:Educational psychologists","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Educational_psychologists","c":[]},{"n":"Category:Educational researchers","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Educational_researchers","c":[]},{"n":"Category:Educational theorists","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Educational_theorists","c":[]},{"n":"Category:Historians of education","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Historians_of_education","c":[]},{"n":"Category:Philosophers of education","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Philosophers_of_education","c":[]},{"n":"List of educational psychologists","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_educational_psychologists","c":[]}]},{"n":"Category:Educational personnel","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Educational_personnel","c":[{"n":"Category:Educational administrators","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Educational_administrators","c":[]},{"n":"Category:Educators","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Educators","c":[{"n":"Category:Educators by discipline","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Educators_by_discipline","c":[]}]}]}]},{"n":"Educational technology","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Educational_technology","c":[]},{"n":"Educational theory and practice","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outline_of_education#Educational_theory_and_practice","c":[{"n":"Curriculum studies – field where researchers and educators examine how educational experiences are designed and organized to support meaningful learning","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curriculum_studies","c":[]},{"n":"Educational materials, tools and technologies","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outline_of_education#Educational_materials,_tools_and_technologies","c":[{"n":"Curriculum","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curriculum","c":[]},{"n":"Educational technology (the use of electronic educational technology is also called e-learning)","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Educational_technology","c":[{"n":"Educational animation","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Educational_animation","c":[]},{"n":"Educational robotics","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Educational_robotics","c":[]},{"n":"Outline of open educational resources","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outline_of_education","c":[]}]},{"n":"Instructional materials","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instructional_materials","c":[{"n":"Lesson plan","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lesson_plan","c":[]},{"n":"Textbook","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Textbook","c":[]}]}]},{"n":"Educational research – systematic collection and analysis of evidence and data related to various aspects of education including student learning, interaction, teaching methods, teacher training, and classroom dynamics.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Educational_research","c":[]},{"n":"Instructional theory – study of how to design learning environments, methods, and materials in ways that facilitate learning","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instructional_theory","c":[]},{"n":"Learning theory – describes how students receive, process, and retain knowledge during learning.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Learning_theory_(education)","c":[]},{"n":"Pedogagical and instructional approaches","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outline_of_education#Pedogagical_and_instructional_approaches","c":[{"n":"Alternative education","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alternative_education","c":[]},{"n":"Instructional design","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instructional_design","c":[]},{"n":"Learning community","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Learning_community","c":[]},{"n":"Learning environment","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Learning_environment","c":[]},{"n":"Learning space","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Learning_space","c":[]},{"n":"Learning styles","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Learning_styles","c":[]},{"n":"Socialization","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Socialization","c":[]},{"n":"Teaching methods","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outline_of_education#Teaching_methods","c":[{"n":"Collaborative learning – situation in which two or more people learn or attempt to learn something together.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collaborative_learning","c":[]},{"n":"Context-based learning – use of real-life and fictitious examples in teaching environments in order to learn through the actual, practical experience with a subject","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Context-based_learning","c":[]},{"n":"Design-based learning – inquiry-based form of learning, or pedagogy, that is based on integration of design thinking and the design process into the classroom at the K-12 and post-secondary levels.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Design-based_learning","c":[]},{"n":"Direct instruction – explicit teaching of a skill set using lectures or demonstrations of the material to students","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Direct_instruction","c":[]},{"n":"Evidence-based education – principle that education practices should be based on the best available scientific evidence, with randomised trials as the gold standard of evidence, rather than tradition, personal judgement, or other influences","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evidence-based_education","c":[]},{"n":"Experiential education – philosophy of education that describes the process that occurs between a teacher and student that infuses direct experience with the learning environment and content","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Experiential_education","c":[]},{"n":"Experiential learning – process of learning through experience, and is more narrowly defined as \"learning through reflection on doing\"","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Experiential_learning","c":[]},{"n":"Homework set of tasks assigned to students by their teachers to be completed at home.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homework","c":[]},{"n":"Inquiry-based learning – form of active learning that starts by posing questions, problems or scenarios.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inquiry-based_learning","c":[]},{"n":"Kinesthetic learning – learning that involves physical activity, preferring whole-body movement to process new and difficult information.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinesthetic_learning","c":[]},{"n":"Learning by teaching – method of teaching in which students are made to learn material and prepare lessons to teach it to the other students.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Learning_by_teaching","c":[]},{"n":"Online learning community – public or private destination on the Internet that addresses its members' learning needs by facilitating peer-to-peer learning","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Online_learning_community","c":[]},{"n":"Open classroom – student-centered learning space design format, where large group of students of varying skill levels would be in a single, large classroom with several teachers overseeing them.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_classroom","c":[]},{"n":"Open learning – activities that either enhance learning opportunities within formal education systems or broaden learning opportunities beyond formal education systems","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_learning","c":[]},{"n":"Outcome-based education – educational theory that bases each part of an educational system around goals (outcomes)","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outcome-based_education","c":[]},{"n":"Outdoor education – organized learning that takes place in the outdoors, such as during school camping trips","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outdoor_education","c":[]},{"n":"Personalized learning – efforts to tailor education to meet the different needs of students.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personalized_learning","c":[]},{"n":"Problem-based learning – teaching method in which students learn about a subject through the experience of solving an open-ended problem found in trigger material","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Problem-based_learning","c":[]},{"n":"Problem-posing education – method of teaching that emphasizes critical thinking for the purpose of liberation, coined by Brazilian educator Paulo Freire","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Problem-posing_education","c":[]},{"n":"Project-based learning – teaching method that involves a dynamic classroom approach in which it is believed that students acquire a deeper knowledge through active exploration of real-world challenges and problems","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project-based_learning","c":[]},{"n":"Service-learning – educational approach that uses community service to meet both classroom learning objectives and societal needs","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Service-learning","c":[]},{"n":"Single-sex education – practice of conducting education with male and female students attending separate classes, buildings or schools","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single-sex_education","c":[]},{"n":"Slow education – adaptive and non-standards based approaches to teaching","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slow_education","c":[]},{"n":"Student-centred learning – methods of teaching that shift the focus of instruction from the teacher to the student, aiming to develop learner autonomy and independence","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Student-centred_learning","c":[]},{"n":"Taxonomy of Educational Objectives (Bloom's Taxonomy) – framework for categorizing educational goals, developed by a committee of educators chaired by Benjamin Bloom in 1956","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taxonomy_of_Educational_Objectives","c":[]}]}]}]},{"n":"History of education","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outline_of_education#History_of_education","c":[{"n":"Category:History of education by country","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:History_of_education_by_country","c":[]},{"n":"History of early childhood care and education","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_early_childhood_care_and_education","c":[]},{"n":"History of education","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_education","c":[]},{"n":"History of higher education","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Higher_education#History","c":[]}]},{"n":"Participants in education","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outline_of_education#Participants_in_education","c":[{"n":"Catechist","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catechist","c":[]},{"n":"Chancellor","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chancellor_(education)","c":[]},{"n":"Dean","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dean_(education)","c":[]},{"n":"Parent (via parenting ) – students' parents typically play a large role in teaching their children and overseeing their formal education, often including financing it.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parent","c":[]},{"n":"Principal (academia)","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Principal_(academia)","c":[]},{"n":"Rector","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rector_(academia)","c":[]},{"n":"School counselor","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/School_counselor","c":[]},{"n":"School psychologist","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/School_psychologist","c":[]},{"n":"Student – person enrolled in a school or other educational institution, or more generally, a person who takes a special interest in a subject.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Student","c":[]},{"n":"Teacher – person who helps students to acquire knowledge, competence, or virtue, via the practice of teaching.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teacher","c":[{"n":"Head teacher (Principal) – staff member of a school with the greatest responsibility[1] for the management of the school.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Head_teacher","c":[]},{"n":"Professor – academic rank at universities and other post-secondary education and research institutions in most countries, usually denoting an expert in his/her field and a teacher of the highest rank","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Professor","c":[{"n":"Adjunct professor – bona-fide part-time non-tenure faculty member in an adjunct position at an institution of higher education.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adjunct_professor","c":[]},{"n":"Assistant professor – academic rank just below the rank of an associate professor used in universities or colleges, mainly in the United States, Canada, Japan, and South Korea.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assistant_professor","c":[]},{"n":"Associate professor – academic rank between assistant professor and a full professorship (North America)","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Associate_professor","c":[]},{"n":"Lecturer – academic rank in the commonwealth system, denoting a teaching position higher than that of the entry-level associate lecturer.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lecturer","c":[]}]},{"n":"Teacher's assistant – individual who assists a professor or teacher with instructional responsibilities.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teacher%27s_assistant","c":[]},{"n":"Tutor – person who provides personalized assistance or tutelage to one or more people on certain subject areas or skills","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tutor","c":[]}]}]},{"n":"Types of educational goals and outcomes","u":"https